#### Operation Summary Example of a File Transfer

A file transfer adds four rows to the summary: source files, target path, target file name (absent unless a rename was requested) and transfer mode, all taken from the pre-check result. The transfer mode is the one **actually in effect**, and the "[Default]" mark only shows up when `transfer_mode` was not passed, never when the caller passed it explicitly. Every row carrying the "[Default]" prefix (including the transfer mode when it took effect by default) is placed at the end of the summary table, after the ordinary field rows. The rendered table looks like:

```markdown
| Item | Content |
| --- | --- |
| Operation type | Fast transfer file |
| Resource scope | BlueKing(biz:2) |
| Initiator | admin |
| Risk level | Low |
| Operation object | Distribute the installation package |
| Execute account | root |
| Source files | root: /pkg/app.tar.gz,/pkg/conf.yaml; mysql: /pkg/db.sql |
| Target path | /data/app/ |
| Transfer mode | Safety mode (store files with the same name in directories named by source IP, create the target path if it does not exist) |
| Total execute objects | 3 |
| [Default] Timeout | 7200s |
```

A job plan containing file transfer steps works the same way: the source files and target paths of every step are de-duplicated and merged into one row. A row lists at most 5 entries; anything beyond that is not expanded and only the total is appended:

```markdown
| Item | Content |
| --- | --- |
| Operation type | Execute job plan |
| Resource scope | BlueKing(biz:2) |
| Initiator | admin |
| Risk level | Medium |
| Operation object | Release process |
| Execute account | root |
| Source files | root: /pkg/app-0.tar.gz; root: /pkg/app-1.tar.gz; root: /pkg/app-2.tar.gz; root: /pkg/app-3.tar.gz; root: /pkg/app-4.tar.gz; the rest omitted (7 in total) |
| Target path | /data/app/ |
| Transfer mode | Force mode (overwrite files with the same name, create the target path if it does not exist) |
| Job plan ID | 1000 |
| Total execute objects | 12 |
```

### Response Parameters

{% include '_generic_v4_response.md.j2' %}

#### data

| Field | Type | Description |
|-------|------|-------------|
| approval_task_id | string | Approval task ID |
| expire_at | long | Expiry moment of the approval task, Unix timestamp in milliseconds. After expiry the task can no longer be released |
| approval_content | string | Approval content in Markdown, already rendered by the Job platform, which the channel can present directly. It contains, in order, a level-one title (such as "Fast execute script - BlueKing - 37 execute object(s)"), the summary table (operation type, resource scope, initiator, risk level, operation object, execute account, source files, target path, target file name, transfer mode, total execute objects, defaults that took effect, and so on), the script content code block (tagged with the script language, such as shell or python, so the channel can highlight it) and the raw parameters JSON code block. The execute account is the de-duplicated set of account aliases resolved for every step. The source files, target path, target file name and transfer mode rows only show up when the operation has file transfer steps (including a job plan that contains them), and each is the de-duplicated set of what the pre-check resolved for every step. The source files are given as "execute account: file path" and do not list the source hosts (to check which hosts the files are taken from, look at the raw parameters in the body for a fast transfer file, or at the step definition of the job plan). The target file name appears only when the caller asked for a rename, and the transfer mode is the **mode actually in effect** (strict / force / safety mode) together with how files with the same name and a missing target path are handled, marked with "[Default]" only when `transfer_mode` was not passed and the force mode took effect by default. Every row carrying the "[Default]" prefix is placed at the end of the summary table, after the ordinary field rows. Each of these merged rows lists at most 5 entries; anything beyond that is not expanded and only "the rest omitted (N in total)" is appended. The "Operation" row of a save cron operation reads "Create" or "Update", following the language of the request. Per-step details (step name, script name, target host list, and so on) are not expanded in the body, so that an over-long ticket does not push the approver to scroll straight to the bottom and release it. High-risk items that must stand out (matched high-risk statement rules, dynamic group targets) are rendered in Markdown bold. Fields that were not provided are omitted from the raw parameters, which therefore list only what the caller actually passed in. The values of password-like sensitive fields are placeholders only and carry no real value, **script content being the single exception shown as is**. When the target contains dynamic groups or topology nodes, the body states that the machines actually executed on are resolved again at release time |
